      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Tarrowby Logistics, a fictional company. First you're on the red team: look at what Tarrowby has posted about its open jobs, then write one job application to its recruiter, Fatima Begum, with an attachment picked from a list of labels. Nothing real is attached or sent. Then you swap seats and sort a recruitment inbox like the one your email landed in. Only the inbox is scored.",
        "flipHeading": "Now it lands on you",
        "flipIntro": "This is the application you just sent, the way it looked to Fatima, Tarrowby's recruitment lead. The parts picked out below are what make it believable.",
        "flipDefenderLine": "Fatima opened it between two interviews, forty applications still unread. One more CV for the warehouse job, from someone who named the role, so she opened the attachment. Forty applications deep, anyone opens the next one.",
        "flipDefenderLineByChoice": {
          "pretext_application": "Fatima opened it between two interviews with forty applications still unread. It named the warehouse vacancy she was recruiting for and carried a CV, so it read as one more real applicant and she opened the attachment.",
          "pretext_general": "A speculative 'in case anything comes up' note names no role, so it has less pull, yet a quiet CV from a stranger still gets opened on a busy day. Whatever the covering line, the attachment is the part that does the work."
        },
        "flipNoSend": "Your application didn't go out this time, so there's nothing of yours to show. Next, the same kind of message arrives in a recruitment inbox, and you're the one opening it.",
        "flipMissed": "The defence is the same either way: next, the same kind of message lands in a recruitment inbox, and you're the one opening it.",
        "flipMissedHeading": "Nothing to replay this time",
        "flipLandedNoTells": "It opened like any real application, with nothing off to pick up on. Next, the same kind of message lands in a recruitment inbox, and you're the one opening it.",
        "noBlameLine": "Every one of these was built to look right, and opening attachments from strangers is a recruiter's whole job. A miss here points to a busy inbox, and to where to look next time.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: read CVs in the viewer, and if a document asks you to enable editing or content, don't. If a CV 'doesn't come through' and sits behind a link that wants you to sign in, ask the candidate to send it as an attachment instead. Then report anything odd using the route in the box below.",
        "defendOnly": "These were built by people who know exactly what a real application looks like, and opening a CV is a recruiter's job, not a lapse. Any you were caught out by are shown below, with what gave each one away.",
        "conceptPanel": {
          "heading": "Why 'enable content' matters",
          "text": "Office documents can carry macros, small programs that run inside the file. Many organisations now block macros in files that arrive from the internet, so this trick works less often than it used to. The lure still turns up, though, because it only has to work once. Reading a CV in the viewer is always enough; a real CV never needs you to switch anything on."
        }
      },
